D-029 blocks public untrusted-input release while OxJSONLD runs in process without
hard cancellation or internal allocation and diagnostic budgets. ADR 0014 governs
native qualification across Linux, macOS, and Windows: each advertised target needs a
real killable worker, a tested target-specific memory mechanism, bounded deadlines and
fail-stop cleanup, host-wide bounded concurrency and queueing, bounded
request/response transfer, orphan prevention, and no in-process fallback.
Evidence-harness values are not product defaults. Browser and
Node hostile input require separately qualified replaceable Workers with deadlines,
bounded concurrency, and bounded request/response transfer. In-process parsing may
not be presented as resource-bounded.
